From Late {{inh|en|enm|man of wer}}, {{m|enm|man of werre|t=fighting man, soldier}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|subentry=man of [wer(re]|entry=wer(re|pos=n|id=MED52312}}</ref> It has been suggested that {{senseno|en|warship}} (“powerful armed naval vessel”) derives from the fact that such vessels were manned by men-of-war (“soldiers”; {{senseno|en|soldier}}).<ref>See, for example, {{cite-book|author=Eliezer Edwards|entry=Man-of-war|title=Words, Facts, and Phrases: A Dictionary of Curious, Quaint, & Out-of-the-way Matters|location=London|publisher={{w|Chatto & Windus}},{{nb...|Piccadilly}}|year=1882|page=351|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7DcCAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA351|oclc=174082463}}.</ref>

